+# Decision: Concurrency Mechanism for Competing Visit Edits
+
+## Context
+
+A visit can be edited by more than one worker before either edit reaches the server, because tablets work offline for up to 48 hours and workers sometimes correct the same visit independently after comparing field notes. The record store must turn whatever arrives into a defensible current state without treating arrival order as evidence of authority, and without discarding any submitted edit. How it does so determines what tablet-sync must retain locally and what review-export must present to coordinators, so getting it wrong is expensive to unwind once those two nodes are built against it.
+
+Every option in contention preserves every original submission; that constraint is inherited from the project's tenet against silently discarding a submitted edit and does not distinguish between the options below.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+- **Scientific-integrity risk of automatic combination** (major) — a visit's fields can be scientifically coupled (a species identification and its cover estimate) in ways the server cannot infer from field names. A mechanism that combines edits automatically risks producing a record that is factually wrong in a way no one chose, with no review step to catch it. This turns on the tenet of keeping the path from submission to export inspectable, and on the project's non-goal of not inferring scientific intent.
+- **Coordinator review workload** (major) — the team runs review with two coordinators and no dedicated systems administrator. A mechanism that routes more concurrent edits to manual review costs operator time every week the project runs.
+- **Rule-maintenance burden** (moderate) — some mechanisms require standing logic that must anticipate every field's combination behavior and be revisited as the visit schema changes; others require none.
+- **Export eligibility** (moderate) — the partner receives a reviewed CSV every Friday. A mechanism that resolves more concurrent edits automatically makes more visits eligible for that export sooner; one that holds more edits for review shrinks the export set and grows the backlog.
+- **Outcome explainability to the submitting worker** (minor) — tablet-sync must explain server outcomes to field workers. A mechanism whose outcome is one of a small number of whole-visit states is easier to explain than one where part of a worker's submission can survive and part can be silently superseded.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-Visit Revisions
+
+Each edit names the revision it started from. Edits that start from the same revision become competing heads and sit side by side, unresolved, until a coordinator picks one or folds them together by hand. The visit is never split or recombined by the server itself.
+
+- **Scientific-integrity risk**: strong fit — nothing is ever combined without a coordinator's explicit choice, so no automatic step can cross a scientific relationship it can't see.
+- **Coordinator review workload**: weak fit — two edits that touch entirely unrelated fields still produce competing heads and still need a coordinator's attention, even though nothing about them actually conflicts.
+- **Rule-maintenance burden**: strong fit — there is no per-field combination logic to write or maintain, now or as the schema grows.
+- **Export eligibility**: weak fit — any visit with competing heads is excluded from the Friday export regardless of which fields the competing edits touch, so the export set shrinks and the backlog grows faster than the underlying scientific conflict rate would justify.
+- **Outcome explainability**: strong fit — a worker's submission is always in one of two states, current or pending against a competitor; nothing about it is silently altered.
+
+Summary: trades review volume and export latency for a guarantee that no two edits are ever combined without a human choosing to.
+
+### Field-Aware Three-Way Merge
+
+Each edit supplies its base state and its changes. Changes to disjoint fields combine automatically into a new current state; changes that overlap the same field go to a coordinator for review.
+
+- **Scientific-integrity risk**: weak fit — the merge logic decides what is safe to combine by field identity, not by scientific relationship, so it will automatically combine two edits whose fields don't overlap even when the underlying observations they represent are coupled (a species identification corrected in one edit, its cover estimate corrected in another).
+- **Coordinator review workload**: strong fit — only edits that overlap the same field reach a coordinator; everything else resolves without review.
+- **Rule-maintenance burden**: weak fit — needs a maintained combination rule for every field shape in the visit (scalar fields, the observer list, the species-entry collection), each revisited whenever the visit schema changes.
+- **Export eligibility**: strong fit — more visits clear automatically and become eligible for the Friday export sooner.
+- **Outcome explainability**: weak fit — a worker's submission can be partly present and partly silently superseded depending on which fields another edit happened to touch, which tablet-sync would have to reconstruct and explain field by field rather than as one outcome.
+
+Summary: trades a smaller review queue and faster export eligibility for the chance of silently combining edits that are scientifically related but happen to land on different fields, plus an open-ended rule-maintenance obligation.
+
+## Selection
+
+Whole-visit revisions is selected. It wins the two major criteria: it cannot silently combine edits whose scientific relationship the server has no way to see, and it keeps review simple enough for two coordinators to run without a dedicated systems administrator maintaining a growing set of field-combination rules. This is a direct application of the project's inspectable-path tenet and its non-goal against inferring scientific intent — field-aware merge's core mechanism is exactly the kind of inference the project has ruled out of scope.
+
+The decision is taken against the project's moderate-weight interest in a smaller review queue and faster export eligibility, which field-aware merge would have won. That cost is accepted because it is visible and absorbable — a larger queue and a later export date for some visits is a cost the team can see and plan around — where a scientifically invalid auto-merge would be invisible until someone downstream noticed the record was wrong.
+
+Committing to this forecloses ever auto-combining disjoint edits without a future decision that explicitly revisits this doc. It does not foreclose a coordinator from incorporating fields from more than one competing revision by hand when resolving a visit — that stays a per-visit human judgment call, not a standing automatic rule, so it does not reopen the scientific-integrity risk this decision avoids.
+
+It obligates:
+
+- **Tablet-sync** to retain base revision IDs locally, to surface a "your edit is pending against a competitor" outcome distinct from "your edit is now current," and to handle revision chains that keep growing for visits that are disputed repeatedly.
+- **Review-export** to provide a revision-comparison view for coordinators, to exclude any visit with competing heads from the Friday export regardless of which fields the competing edits touch, and to define "ready for export" as single-head visits only — accepting a smaller export set and a larger unresolved queue than field-aware merge would have produced.
+
+Revisit this decision if the review queue becomes operationally unworkable for two coordinators and the project becomes willing to trade some risk of unnoticed field-level auto-combination to relieve it. If that happens, the rule-maintenance burden and the explainability cost above are the first things to re-examine against whatever review capacity the team has at that point.
